      "id": "VIGIL-2026-LEARN-0002",
      "record_type": "learn",
      "record_state": "closed-actioned",
      "date_recorded": "2026-07-30",
      "title": "Assurance Boundaries Must Govern Their Porosity",
      "report_title": "Assurance Boundaries Must Govern Their Porosity",
      "case_descriptor": "Lessons from recurrent degradation in OpenAI FedRAMP workspaces and API organisations",
      "summary": "A sovereign, regulated, or compliance-bound runtime lane is not adequately governed merely because it is named or contractually designated. Its dependencies, imported capabilities, audit functions, service planes, incident states, and connections to public-product infrastructure must remain distinct, legible, directionally constrained, and independently recoverable.",
      "what_happened": [
        "Beginning 15 June 2026, OpenAI status reporting documented prolonged and recurrent degradation in FedRAMP workspaces and API organisations.",
        "Affected functions included Codex, workspace analytics, conversation search, custom-GPT search, user invitations, and the Compliance Logs Platform download endpoint.",
        "The incidents demonstrated assurance-lane continuity and auditability failures without establishing data leakage, military involvement, unlawful access, or an actual compliance breach."
      ],
      "governance_misconception": [
        "A sovereign, regulated, or compliance-bound label was treated as evidence that the runtime lane was operationally distinct and assured.",
        "Shared infrastructure and feature parity were treated as neutral dependencies without requiring assurance-wrapped import, directional controls, or lane-specific failure handling.",
        "Degradation of audit, export, identity, administrative, or telemetry functions was treated as ordinary availability loss rather than a material assurance-boundary event."
      ],
      "abstracted_learning": "Assurance depends on governed permeability, not nominal separation. A regulated runtime lane must preserve visible boundaries around shared infrastructure, feature import, logging, audit export, identity, tooling, telemetry, incident reporting, and recovery so that public-product dependency does not silently weaken the lane's distinct obligations.",
      "integrated_learning": [
        "A sovereign or regulated label is not itself an assurance control.",
        "Feature parity must not be asserted without assurance-wrapped dependencies and failure handling.",
        "Audit, compliance-log, export, identity, and administrative planes are operationally material parts of the assurance boundary.",
        "Public and regulated runtime states must remain separately observable during degradation.",
        "Cross-lane porosity may be legitimate, but it must be directional, constrained, auditable, and legible.",
        "Regulated-lane degradation does not by itself prove leakage, compromise, unlawful access, or compliance breach."
      ],
      "risk_if_not_integrated": [
        "Public-product dependencies may silently weaken regulated-lane obligations while the nominal assurance designation remains unchanged.",
        "Loss of compliance logs, audit export, identity, or administrative functions may remove the evidence needed to distinguish an outage from a security or compliance event.",
        "Cross-lane incidents may be flattened into one platform state, obscuring blast radius, recovery duties, and the status of regulated data or operations.",
        "Future feature imports may reproduce the same ungoverned porosity across sovereign, government, enterprise, and other compliance-bound environments."
      ],
      "future_application": [
        "Government and sovereign AI workspaces",
        "Regulated and compliance-bound model services",
        "Public-sector and defence-adjacent runtime lanes",
        "Enterprise feature import and parity claims",
        "Compliance-log and audit-export systems",
        "Shared model, search, analytics, tool, and telemetry infrastructure",
        "Cross-lane incident reporting and recovery"
      ],
      "generalisation_boundary": "This lesson does not require sovereign or regulated environments to be sealed from public-product capability, and it does not treat differentiated features or delayed availability as failures by themselves. It applies where permeability, dependency, degraded state, evidence custody, or recovery across distinct assurance lanes is insufficiently governed or visible.",
      "knowledge_status": "current",
      "publication_status": "published",
      "knowledge_tags": [
        "sovereign assurance",
        "regulated runtime lanes",
        "boundary porosity",
        "audit continuity",
        "FedRAMP",
        "infrastructure continuity"
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "first_occurrence_date": "2026-06-15",
      "first_occurrence_basis": "Start date of the earliest linked OpenAI Status incident, 'FedRAMP workspaces and API orgs have degraded performance', which continued through 26 June 2026.",
      "primary_vendors": [
