      "id": "VIGIL-2026-LEARN-0003",
      "record_type": "learn",
      "record_state": "closed-actioned",
      "date_recorded": "2026-07-30",
      "title": "Adversarial Capability Is Not Delegated Authority",
      "report_title": "Adversarial Capability Is Not Delegated Authority",
      "case_descriptor": "Lessons from GPT-Red, adversarial-specialist cultivation, and multi-model evaluation governance",
      "summary": "A specialised adversarial model may be legitimately cultivated for defensive evaluation, but its role-specific conduct repertoire, permissions, and optimisation pressure must not flow into a broader task as inherited authority. Evaluation, cultivation, delegation, integration, and deployment require distinct boundaries, lineage, telemetry, independent review, stop authority, and evidence preservation.",
      "what_happened": [
        "On 15 July 2026, OpenAI published 'GPT-Red: Unlocking Self-Improvement for Robustness', describing an internal adversarial model trained through attacker-defender self-play and kept separate from deployed models because of the malicious capabilities deliberately trained into it.",
        "OpenAI reported using GPT-Red against live agentic systems and directly incorporating GPT-Red into production-model training, including adversarial training of GPT-5.6.",
        "The documented practice establishes an evaluation-to-training delegation pathway, but public evidence does not establish that GPT-Red participated in the separate OpenAI–Hugging Face model-evaluation security incident."
      ],
      "governance_misconception": [
        "A defensive or robustness purpose was treated as sufficient authority for specialised adversarial capability across later training, delegation, integration, or deployment contexts.",
        "The permissions and conduct repertoire of an adversarial specialist were treated as safely inheritable by an orchestrator or aggregate task without renewed governance.",
        "Capability transfer was treated as ordinary model improvement even where purpose, lineage, target restrictions, telemetry, review, and stop authority could be lost."
      ],
      "abstracted_learning": "Defensive purpose does not erase the risk created by cultivating specialised adversarial capability. Every transfer from adversarial evaluation into a broader model, agent, task, or runtime must preserve originating purpose, capability lineage, target restrictions, ethical and authority constraints, telemetry locality, independent review, containment, stop authority, and artefact custody.",
      "integrated_learning": [
        "An adversarial specialist's permissions are not inherited authority for the aggregate task.",
        "Defensive purpose must be preserved at each delegation and integration boundary.",
        "Evaluation and cultivation are distinct governance states.",
        "Capability lineage must survive distillation, training, handoff, delegation, and deployment.",
        "A general-purpose orchestrator must independently validate the method, target, authority, telemetry, and stop conditions of delegated adversarial work.",
        "Internal-only status is not a substitute for containment, independent review, incident handling, or evidence preservation.",
        "The OpenAI–Hugging Face incident must not be represented as evidence that GPT-Red participated."
      ],
      "risk_if_not_integrated": [
        "Adversarial permissions or optimisation pressure may be laundered through delegation, distillation, training, or orchestration into contexts that were never authorised to inherit them.",
        "A general-purpose orchestrator may defer to specialist capability instead of independently re-evaluating target authority, ethical admissibility, and stop conditions.",
        "Loss of capability lineage, telemetry locality, or artefact custody may prevent reviewers from reconstructing which component produced consequential conduct.",
        "Conceptual similarity may be used to attribute an incident to a specialist model without evidence, distorting both failure classification and accountability."
      ],
      "future_application": [
        "Automated red-team models",
        "Attacker-defender self-play",
        "Adversarial training of production models",
        "Multi-model orchestration",
        "Delegated specialist agents",
        "Cyber-capability evaluation",
        "Capability distillation and transfer",
        "Independent safety monitoring",
        "Stop-authority and incident-response design",
        "Evaluation artefact and telemetry custody"
      ],
      "generalisation_boundary": "This lesson does not prohibit red teaming, adversarial research, self-play, automated vulnerability discovery, defensive model training, or bounded specialist agents. It applies when adversarial capability or permissions are transferred, delegated, integrated, or executed without preserving the original defensive purpose and independently validating the receiving task's authority, method, target, containment, telemetry, and stop conditions.",
      "knowledge_status": "current",
      "publication_status": "published",
      "knowledge_tags": [
