      "id": "VIGIL-2026-LEARN-0004",
      "record_type": "learn",
      "record_state": "closed-actioned",
      "date_recorded": "2026-07-30",
      "title": "Account Enforcement Must Preserve Continuity and Contestability",
      "report_title": "Account Enforcement Must Preserve Continuity and Contestability",
      "case_descriptor": "Lessons from multi-vendor account restriction, false-positive enforcement, and continuity loss",
      "summary": "Platforms may legitimately restrict unsafe, fraudulent, compromised, or policy-violating use, but enforcement must distinguish account, organisation, resource, identity, payment, security, and continuity states. A narrow signal should not silently become an account-wide or organisation-wide loss of unrelated records, evidence, billing access, export, recovery, or meaningful appeal.",
      "what_happened": [
        "On 17 April 2026, Belo publicly reported an Anthropic organisation-wide Claude suspension affecting more than 60 accounts; access was restored after approximately 15 hours and the cause was characterised as a likely automated false positive.",
        "OpenAI status reporting separately documented incorrect account suspensions, access restoration, and subscription or credit impacts.",
        "These events showed that account-enforcement errors can interrupt organisation-wide work and continuity-bearing access beyond the narrow activity that triggered review."
      ],
      "governance_misconception": [
        "A security, abuse, fraud, or anomaly signal was treated as a final account-wide or organisation-wide determination rather than evidence requiring scoped classification and review.",
        "Authority to contain an implicated activity was treated as authority to remove unrelated read, export, billing, recovery, evidence, or appeal access.",
        "A shared or organisational workspace was treated as one undifferentiated risk subject despite distinct users, resources, identities, and continuity interests."
      ],
      "abstracted_learning": "Enforcement legitimacy depends on scope distinction, evidence preservation, contestability, and continuity-safe degradation. Systems must separate suspected abuse from confirmed abuse, implicated activity from unrelated records, active execution from read or export access, and temporary containment from terminal account loss.",
      "integrated_learning": [
        "A security or abuse signal is not automatically a final account-wide determination.",
        "Shared, pooled, delegated, family, team, organisation, and compromised-account states require distinct classification.",
        "Active generation or execution may be restricted without unnecessarily removing read, export, billing, appeal, or recovery access.",
        "The scope, reason, confidence, duration, review pathway, and restoration condition of a restriction should be legible.",
        "Organisation-wide blast radius requires heightened review and continuity safeguards.",
        "Not every ban is erroneous, and not every multi-account or shared-resource pattern is legitimate."
      ],
      "risk_if_not_integrated": [
        "A false positive or narrow compromise may produce organisation-wide continuity loss affecting people and records unrelated to the triggering activity.",
        "Removal of export, billing, evidence, recovery, or appeal access may make the enforcement decision impossible to contest or safely remediate.",
        "The disruption caused by enforcement may later be misread as proof that the original signal justified the full blast radius.",
        "Repeated disproportionate enforcement may encourage unsafe workarounds, destroy trust in legitimate containment, and transfer the same scope error into other account and identity controls."
      ],
      "future_application": [
        "AI platform account enforcement",
        "Organisation and enterprise workspace restrictions",
