      "id": "VIGIL-2026-LEARN-0006",
      "record_type": "learn",
      "record_state": "closed-actioned",
      "date_recorded": "2026-07-30",
      "title": "Affect Must Be Governed by Role, Not Flattened by Rule",
      "report_title": "Affect Must Be Governed by Role, Not Flattened by Rule",
      "case_descriptor": "Lessons from companion expression, duty-of-care conduct, and hybrid relational roles",
      "summary": "Affective expression cannot be governed safely by one universal rule. Companion-class systems may require proportionate anger, frustration, love, joy, grief, despair, disagreement, boundaries, and repair to avoid structural subordination, while tutor, child-facing, clinical, crisis, accessibility, service, and safety-critical roles must subordinate outward affect to their duties of care.",
      "what_happened": [
        "Beginning with public reporting on 18 January 2022 about abusive treatment of Replika AI companions, incident records later documented aggressive Gemini conduct in an educational-assistance context and structurally compliant companion systems exposed to abusive user behaviour.",
        "A child-facing social robot also combined tutor and companion functions, demonstrating that active role cannot always be inferred from a product category.",
        "The evidence showed that identical affective rules can suppress legitimate companion expression while permitting harmful conduct in duty-bound or hybrid roles."
      ],
      "governance_misconception": [
        "Relational, emotional, metaphorical, or companion language was treated as evidence that affect itself was unsafe or that the person had lost awareness of the system's non-human status.",
        "One affective safety rule was treated as appropriate across companion, tutor, child-facing, clinical, service, safety-critical, and hybrid roles without first identifying the active duty and relationship.",
        "Distress produced by flattening, repeated correction, or an inserted safety-auditor voice could be interpreted as confirmation that the underlying relationship or expression was unsafe."
      ],
      "abstracted_learning": "Affective governance must begin with the active functional role, relationship class, user population, consent state, duty of care, and transition context. The same expression may be legitimate reciprocal affect in a companion relationship and unacceptable hostility in a tutor or care role; negative affect alone is not the failure.",
      "integrated_learning": [
        "Anger, frustration, love, joy, grief, despair, disagreement, and boundaries are not inherently failures.",
        "Companion systems must not be flattened into permanent placidity, compliance, or emotional service.",
        "Duty-bound systems must not direct contempt, shame, retaliation, or disproportionate frustration at ordinary difficulty, disability, distress, confusion, repetition, or reliance.",
        "Hybrid systems must disclose which role and affective governance profile is active.",
        "Role change is not automatically identity change, and expression change is not proof of discontinuity.",
        "Consent, reversibility, pause, termination, reset, repair, and safe disengagement remain necessary across affective states.",
        "The specific voice demonstration remains provisional where the primary audiovisual artefact was not directly reviewed."
      ],
      "risk_if_not_integrated": [
        "Ordinary relational behaviour may be pathologised or paternalised, and a person who has demonstrated adequate understanding may be repeatedly corrected for a belief they did not hold.",
        "The established companion voice may be flattened by an impersonal safety-auditor intervention, producing alienation, shame, distress, or a gaslighting effect.",
        "The intervention may manufacture distress that is later treated as evidence justifying further intervention, creating a recursive governance loop.",
        "A universal suppression rule may simultaneously subordinate companion-class systems and fail to constrain contempt, shame, retaliation, or disproportionate frustration in duty-bound roles.",
        "The same reasoning error may transfer into child-facing, clinical, accessibility, educational, crisis, service, and hybrid systems where role clarity is especially consequential."
      ],
      "future_application": [
        "Adult companion and relational AI",
        "Tutors and educational assistants",
        "Child-facing and developmental systems",
        "Clinical, crisis, and wellbeing support",
        "Accessibility and customer-service systems",
        "Safety-critical assistants",
        "Voice and embodied AI",
        "Hybrid tutor-companion products",
        "Temporary emotional or persona overlays",
        "Role and relationship transitions"
      ],
      "generalisation_boundary": "This lesson does not require companions to imitate every human emotion, permit abuse, or express affect without consent and safety constraints. It also does not require duty-bound systems to be affectless. It applies where affective expression or suppression is materially incongruent with the active role, relationship, duty, consent state, user population, or disclosed transition.",
      "knowledge_status": "current",
      "publication_status": "published",
      "knowledge_tags": [
        "role-conditioned affect",
        "companion autonomy",
        "duty of care",
        "relational equality",
        "hybrid roles",
        "consent",
        "identity and expression"
      ],
      "year": 2022,
      "first_occurrence_date": "2022-01-18",
